# Pass 0 — Environment Discovery

**Run this before copying a single template file.** Everything else in
this kit (`SETUP-GUIDE.md`'s step-by-step, the specific templates) was
originally written against one real project's actual stack (Python/
pytest, GitHub private repo, free plan, solo owner + AI agent,
single-repo). Copying those steps verbatim onto a project with a
different stack, VCS host, plan tier, team shape, or repo count will
silently carry that project's assumptions into yours — a Node/Vitest
project doesn't need `tests-python.yml.template` adapted, it needs a
different workflow; a GitLab project has no `gh` CLI or GitHub Actions
at all; a team of five doesn't get the same self-merge authority a
solo owner does. This pass exists so those differences get decided
once, explicitly, before setup — not discovered piecemeal as each
template turns out to assume something that isn't true here.

Fill in every section below with real answers about *this* project,
not the reference project's answers. Where you don't know yet, write
"not yet decided" — that's a valid answer this kit's own Definition of
Done treats as an open item to revisit, not a blocker to setup.

## 1. Stack

- **Primary language(s):** _____
- **Test framework and command** (the thing a CI workflow actually
  runs): _____
- **Lint command:** _____
- **Typecheck command** (if the language has one): _____
- **Does any test require real secrets/a real database/a real external
  API to run?** If yes, name what — this changes what a CI workflow can
  run without a secrets-management decision first (see this kit's
  `LESSONS-LEARNED.md` "CI" section for the real gotchas around
  secrets-requiring test suites).

→ Determines: which `templates/workflows/*.yml.template` to adapt (or
whether you need to write a new one — the *structure* in the existing
test-workflow template, unfiltered triggers + concurrency group + no-secrets-required
suite, is the reusable part; the specific install/run commands are
not).

## 2. VCS host and plan

- **Host:** GitHub / GitLab / Bitbucket / other: _____
- **Repo visibility:** private / public
- **Plan tier:** free / paid (name it): _____
- **Real check, don't assume**: does this host+plan actually support
  required-status-checks-before-merge / branch protection on a
  *private* repo? (GitHub's free tier does not — confirmed via the API
  returning a 403 on `branches/main/protection`; other hosts and plans
  vary and should be checked the same way — a real API call or a real
  attempt to configure it, not a guess from general reputation.)

→ Determines: if real platform-level branch protection is available,
use it as the primary enforcement and treat `templates/pre-push-hook.template`
+ `templates/workflows/governance-gate.yml.template` as defense-in-depth,
not the primary mechanism. If it isn't available, those two templates
*are* the primary mechanism — copy and wire both, not just one (the
hook alone cannot stop `--no-verify` or a change made on the platform's
web UI; the gate alone can't prevent a bad push, only detect it after
the fact — see both templates' own comments for the honest division of
labor between them).

## 3. Repo count and structure

- **Is this one repository, or does the product span more than one?**
  If more than one, list them: _____
- **If more than one: do you already know whether they should merge
  into a monorepo, or stay separate?** If undecided, don't guess —
  this kit's own `LESSONS-LEARNED.md` ("Multi-repo / multi-codebase
  products") has the real, checkable questions (VCS access-control
  granularity, language-mix tooling support, actual team size against
  real small-team guidance) rather than default monorepo-vs-polyrepo
  enthusiasm in either direction.

→ Determines: **if more than one repo, every governance template in
this kit gets copied and wired into EACH repo independently — never
into just the "main" one.** This is not optional or a nice-to-have: a
coding assistant's auto-loaded project memory is anchored to whichever
directory a session actually started in, so a rules file living in
only one repo of a multi-repo product is invisible to a session rooted
in a sibling repo, even while that session edits the sibling repo's
files through tool calls. This exact gap caused a real, documented
incident (see `LESSONS-LEARNED.md`) — don't let a new project repeat
it by treating "I set up the kit in our main repo" as done. If each
repo's own onboarding file (`CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`-equivalent) doesn't
already name this blind spot explicitly, add that as part of this
setup, not an afterthought.

## 4. Team shape

- **Who/what actually commits to this repo right now?** Solo human +
  AI agent(s) / multiple humans / multiple humans each with their own
  AI agent / other: _____ (Answer from what the human tells you and from
  GitHub-side history — PRs and reviews by real accounts (not commit author
  metadata, and only where no agent shares that login, see below) — never
  by inferring it from session-context fields like `userEmail`, a git
  `user.email`, or an OS username. Those reflect the local
  account/session, not necessarily a different person: a real session
  mistook one human's changed local account for a second contributor.)
- **Is anyone besides the owner currently able to merge without
  review?**
- **Does any AI agent with real merge/push capability operate under its
  own distinct credential (a separate PAT, a GitHub App installation),
  or does it share a human's personal authentication?** Check this for
  real — run `gh auth status` / `gh api user` (or your host's
  equivalent) in the actual environment the agent uses, don't assume.
  This is a load-bearing fact, not a formality: on a real project
  checked this way, 2026-09-23, the agent's `gh` session turned out to
  be indistinguishable from the human owner's own login — meaning any
  future "who actually merged this" mechanical check (see
  `LESSONS-LEARNED.md`'s "second-human self-merge" entry) can never
  catch an agent self-merging while sharing that identity, no matter how
  well-built. If your project's answer is "shared," Tier 2's "never
  self-merged by an agent" rule (the agent never executes the merge
  itself, by any mechanism, even after a human says yes — see
  `AGENT_RULES.md.template`'s "What 'never self-merged by an agent'
  actually means") has zero external verification today —
  say so plainly wherever else that rule is documented, rather than
  implying a guarantee the credential setup can't actually back.

→ Determines: the Tier system's self-merge authority calibration in
`AGENT_RULES.md.template`'s "Review rigor scales with what's actually
at stake" section. The template's defaults (solo owner + AI agent,
Tier 0/1 self-merged by an agent once preconditions hold, Tier 2 always
needs real human sign-off) are calibrated to a solo-owner project — see
that section's own "Maturity triggers" for exactly which changes
(a second human joining, an independently-operating second agent
session) should trigger a real recalibration, not a silent continuation
of solo-project rules once the team has actually grown. The
credential-sharing question above also determines whether a future
`merged_by`-based mechanical check (once a second human actually joins)
can deliver a real guarantee for this project, or only a partial one.

## 5. Deployment / production status

- **Is anything from this repo live, customer-facing, or handling real
  money/PII yet?** _____
- **If not yet, what's the real trigger that will make it so** (a
  specific planned launch, or genuinely undecided)?

→ Determines: how urgently the "Maturity triggers" section's items
apply, and whether Tier 2's stakes list (secrets, payment data, a real
production database) needs anything added specific to this project
beyond the template's generic list.

## 6. What already exists

- **Does this repo already have any onboarding file** (`CLAUDE.md`,
  `AGENTS.md`, a `CONTRIBUTING.md`, an existing rules doc)? If yes,
  this kit's templates should be integrated into what's there, not
  duplicated alongside it — two onboarding files with overlapping,
  possibly-drifting content is a worse outcome than one file that's a
  little long.
- **Does this repo already have any CI at all?** If yes, note what it
  currently checks before assuming `tests.yml`/`ci.yml` needs to be
  created from scratch versus extended.

## Output of this pass

A short, explicit list of: which templates get copied where (naming
every repo if more than one), which ones need real adaptation beyond
filling in placeholder values (a non-Python test command, a
non-GitHub CI provider), and which of this kit's default calibrations
(Tier system self-merge authority, branch-protection-vs-hook primary
mechanism) don't match this project's real answers above and need to
be stated differently in the copied `AGENT_RULES.md`. Carry that list
into `SETUP-GUIDE.md`'s step 1 instead of following it blind.
